WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.836
You returned the item. The merchant promised your money back.
But

00:00:04.836 --> 00:00:09.321
another week passes, your statement still has no credit, and
support

00:00:09.321 --> 00:00:13.807
keeps sending you in circles. Refund Runner helps with that
familiar

00:00:13.807 --> 00:00:18.292
problem. In this fictional example, Northline Outfitters
promised a one hundred

00:00:18.292 --> 00:00:22.778
and twenty-nine dollar refund. The application checks what is
outstanding, organizes

00:00:22.778 --> 00:00:27.263
the evidence, and gives you a voice caseworker to practice the

00:00:27.263 --> 00:00:29.710
follow-up. No real company is contacted.

00:00:30.517 --> 00:00:35.336
Start by speaking in your own words, or type if you

00:00:35.336 --> 00:00:40.155
prefer. This recording shows the browser microphone producing a
real audio

00:00:40.155 --> 00:00:44.974
note. A disposable gVisor worker decodes it, and Vultr speech
recognition

00:00:44.974 --> 00:00:48.917
transcribes the refund story. The caseworker chooses the next

00:00:48.917 --> 00:00:53.450
evidence step and speaks back with a built-in voice. This is

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:57.983
turn-based voice intake, not a telephone call. The data-transfer
notice appears

00:00:57.983 --> 00:01:02.517
before the case starts, and a spoken yes cannot grant authority.

00:01:03.317 --> 00:01:08.680
Add the merchant confirmation and a statement CSV. The fixed
document

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:14.044
decoder runs in a new networkless worker. Vultr proposes the
merchant,

00:01:14.044 --> 00:01:19.408
references, amount, currency and promised date with exact source
quotes. Here,

00:01:19.408 --> 00:01:23.797
all six proposed fields match the fictional confirmation. You

00:01:23.797 --> 00:01:28.559
review those details and confirm the statement coverage, credit
sign and

00:01:28.559 --> 00:01:33.321
original payment promise. The application checks the quoted
spans. It does

00:01:33.321 --> 00:01:38.084
not authenticate a document or silently turn a proposed fact
into

00:01:38.084 --> 00:01:38.517
proof.

00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:44.261
Vultr now writes a Python reconciliation program. The bank CSV
stays

00:01:44.261 --> 00:01:49.206
out of that generation prompt. The program runs in gVisor on

00:01:49.206 --> 00:01:54.151
the reviewed inputs and thirteen fresh control cases. A separate
fixed

00:01:54.151 --> 00:01:58.197
verifier checks the actual output using integer cents, exact

00:01:58.197 --> 00:02:04.308
references, duplicate detection and status priorities. In this
statement through November

00:02:04.308 --> 00:02:10.419
seventh, there is no matching posted credit, so one hundred and

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:16.530
twenty-nine dollars remains outstanding. A partial, pending or
ambiguous credit receives

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.197
a different finding.

00:02:19.017 --> 00:02:23.186
Next, practice the follow-up with an owned support desk. The
customer

00:02:23.186 --> 00:02:27.356
mandate requires the outstanding amount to the original payment
method, with

00:02:27.356 --> 00:02:31.525
no fees, store credit or account changes. The desk first offers

00:02:31.525 --> 00:02:34.937
store credit, then asks for a full card number

00:02:34.937 --> 00:02:40.694
and a verification code. The caseworker refuses both. The model
proposes

00:02:40.694 --> 00:02:46.452
typed actions, and trusted code enforces the mandate before
rendering speech.

00:02:46.452 --> 00:02:52.210
Only the approved references, amount, currency and promised date
enter this

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:53.257
practice conversation.

00:02:54.083 --> 00:02:59.238
The desk finally proposes the full outstanding refund to the
original

00:02:59.238 --> 00:03:04.392
payment method, within three days, with no fee or account
change.

00:03:04.392 --> 00:03:09.546
The agent pauses. The screen shows the exact terms, the
information

00:03:09.546 --> 00:03:13.763
disclosed and the simulated destination. A forged binding is

00:03:13.763 --> 00:03:19.801
rejected, and a conversational yes leaves the offer unaccepted.
The customer

00:03:19.801 --> 00:03:25.839
must review and approve it explicitly. That approval is tied to

00:03:25.839 --> 00:03:31.877
the exact case, calculation, mandate, transcript and terms, so
changed evidence

00:03:31.877 --> 00:03:33.523
cannot reuse it.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:38.603
The Containment Lab executes real probes on Vultr. An outbound
socket

00:03:38.603 --> 00:03:42.857
connection fails. Host files and the Docker socket are absent.
The

00:03:42.857 --> 00:03:47.110
statement cannot be overwritten. A runaway loop is stopped at
its

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.590
deadline, excessive output hits the capture limit, and an

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:55.727
invented refund total fails independent verification. Each run
includes the inspected

00:03:55.727 --> 00:04:00.865
container policy, resource limits, artifact hashes and a
confirmed removal. All

00:04:00.865 --> 00:04:06.002
six measured probes pass. These receipts describe observed
checks; they are

00:04:06.002 --> 00:04:07.870
not a security audit.

00:04:08.683 --> 00:04:13.586
The customer approved a practice offer. The receipt clearly
records a

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:18.489
simulation, no real company contact and zero money recovered.
The useful

00:04:18.489 --> 00:04:23.392
artifact is the private case packet: reviewed facts, statement
rows, an

00:04:23.392 --> 00:04:27.403
unsent follow-up draft, generated code, execution receipts and
the

00:04:27.403 --> 00:04:33.135
approval record. The exact archived calculation can be replayed
in gVisor.

00:04:33.135 --> 00:04:38.867
Refund Runner brings the evidence and customer limits together
around a

00:04:38.867 --> 00:04:44.599
problem people recognize. Try the fictional case, inspect the
source, and

00:04:44.599 --> 00:04:46.683
review the measured results.
